      <workList xml:id="workDesc_9ac90bbf">
         <work xml:id="work_d1e222219">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_58758411">A1:010</identifier>
            <title xml:id="_19" xml:lang="it">Concerto: Hautbois d'amour, violino 1mo, violino 2do, violoncello et basso continuo</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_c6434f76">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="_21">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <history xml:id="history_22_0">
               <p xml:id="idm148">Scheibe wrote at least two concertos for oboe d’amore – the present one and SchW A1:009 – which survive as manuscripts in the library of the Fürstlich zu Bentheim-Tecklenburgische Musikbibliothek. Count Moritz Casimir I, who reigned 1710–68, played both the flute and cello in the daily court concerts which took place before dinner ('Tafel'). The work is listed in the music library's thematic catalogue begun by Johann Martin Doemming in November 1750. The collection includes manuscripts of works by Georg Philipp Telemann, the Graun brothers (Carl Heinrich and Johann Gottlieb) and Johan Joachim Agrell, among others. As the title of the manuscript mentions 'Scheibe in Copenhagen' the work must therefore have been acquired after 1740 when Scheibe started his employment as Kapellmeister at the Danish court. In his autobiography from 1739 (cf. Mattheson, <rend xml:id="idm149" fontstyle="italic">Grundlage</rend>, 1740; SchW C5:001), Scheibe does not mention having composed concertos for the oboe d'amore. It interesting to note that the title page does not indicate that Scheibe was ‘Kapellmeister’, a title of address he otherwise consistently employed on all works, both music and texts, even after he had retired in 1748.</p>
            </history>
